// @no-instantiate
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: `std:array`'s `mapIndexed` when the CALLBACK'S RESULT `U` is a STRING
// literal union. `vl check` exits 0, `vl build` writes the `.wasm` and reports success, and
// the engine refuses the module at load —
//
//   type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)
//
// (the two `$type`s are different heap types; the disassembly names them apart.)
//
// THE RECEIVER'S ELEMENT TYPE IS IRRELEVANT, which is the whole reason this file exists and
// is written over an `i32[]`. `i32` is on `std/array.vl`'s ADMITTED list, so a reader who
// takes that list at face value concludes this program is fine. It is not. Measured across
// the `T` × `U` grid:
//
//   T = `i32[]`         U = `"a" | "b"`   → invalid module   ← this file
//   T = `string[]`      U = `"a" | "b"`   → invalid module
//   T = `("a"|"b")[]`   U = `"a" | "b"`   → invalid module
//   T = `("a"|"b")[]`   U = `"x" | "y"`   → invalid module (a DIFFERENT union)
//   T = `i32[]`         U = `1 | 2`       → runs, correct    (a NUMERIC union)
//   T = `("a"|"b")[]`   U = `i32`         → runs, correct
//
// So the predicate is `U` alone, and only for the STRING family.
//
// THE MECHANISM is a RESULT-LIST boundary, not the element-read one the rest of this family
// turned out to be. `mapIndexed` builds a `U[]`, and a `U[]` over a literal union is an array
// of interned i32 ATOMS (`ctxKeepsLitUnion` PRESERVES the members at `RC_ELEM`), while the
// callback's declared result `"a" | "b"` SOFTENS to a string ref at `RC_ROOT` — so the value
// the callback hands back and the slot the result list wants disagree, in the same direction
// as every other cell of this family and at a boundary none of the element-read fixes reach.
// The read boundaries were closed by giving three consumers a DECLARATION fallback
// (`tests/cases/soundness/generic-litunion-element-bind-and-store.vl`); this one is the
// `.push` into the freshly built result list, whose element rep comes from the instance's
// `U`, and it has no such consumer yet.
//
// WHY IT IS A `@no-instantiate` AND NOT A FIX HERE: it is a different boundary in a different
// ladder from the element-read family that closed, and the header of `std/array.vl` had it
// mis-scoped ("only when the callback returns the ELEMENT type") until this grid was run. A
// pinned witness is what stops the next rewrite of that header from guessing again.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the point:
// delete it, drop the carve-out from `std/array.vl`'s reach section, and fold a running row
// into the `mapIndexed` coverage.
import { mapIndexed } from "std:array"

function toAB(x: i32, i: i32): "a" | "b" {
  if x + i > 0 { return "b" }
  "a"
}

const ns = [0, 1]
print(ns.mapIndexed(toAB)[1])
